== The Least Kindness Shall Not Lose Its Reward

And so I come to the second and main point of #emph[doctrine.] It rises from the #emph[reward promised] to those who show the least kindness to a #emph[minister] or #emph[righteous man:] though it is only #emph[a cup of cold water, they will never lose their reward.] From this we may observe:

2\. #emph[Doctrine.] The least work of charity shown to a minister or righteous man will be abundantly rewarded, and not only hereafter, but here on earth as well.

Our doing good ought to extend to all who are in want and come within the reach of our knowledge and power, as in Gal 6:10: “As we have opportunity, let us do good to everyone.” Yet here I confine my discourse to #emph[ministers] and #emph[righteous ones,] because they alone are meant by the #emph[little ones] mentioned in my text.

That merciful men will be abundantly rewarded hereafter in heaven for their works of charity, there is no doubt, for it is so clearly expressed in the word of God. Luke 16:9: “Use worldly wealth to make friends for yourselves, so that when it is gone, they will welcome you#super[+] into eternal dwellings.” And 1 Tim 6:17: “Instruct those who are rich in the present age … to do good, to be rich in good works, and to be generous and ready to share, treasuring up for themselves a firm foundation” for the time to come, that they may lay hold of eternal life.

But the great question concerns #emph[temporal reward: whether Christian charity, rightly performed, will be rewarded here in this life with temporal blessings.] That is, is there ground to expect that what men give by way of charity will be returned here into their bosoms again with increase?

I do not deny that a charitable man may become poor and be brought into some straits. This may come through standing surety for others, through negligence in his calling, or because of some secret sin he lives in, which may justly bring a temporal curse on him. So in such cases his liberality may not be a sufficient fence against want and poverty. But this I say: #emph[as stinginess toward the poor is the readiest way to poverty, so Christian charity, rightly performed, is the surest way to plenty and abundance.] It is usually rewarded with #emph[temporal] blessings here, as well as with #emph[eternal] blessings hereafter. Where is the man to be found who is the poorer for what he has given to the poor? How many are the instances of those who have met with some #emph[signal blessings] from God in this life as the reward of their liberality!

To many uncharitable men this may seem a #emph[paradox,] that #emph[giving should be the surest way of getting,] and that #emph[the more liberal any man is, the more likely he is to thrive and prosper in the world.] Yet nothing is more clearly laid down in the Scripture, or found to be true by the experience of multitudes of God’s people in all ages. #emph[And I am truly persuaded that there is seldom any man who makes conscience of this duty, and gives out to the poor in proportion to what God has bestowed on him, and with an honest, upright heart, who will not find what he gave doubled and redoubled on him in temporal blessings, if he observes the dealings of God’s providence toward him.] I dare challenge all the world to give one instance, or at least any considerable number of instances, of any truly merciful men whose charity has ruined them. But as #emph[living wells] spring and flow the more freely the more they are drawn, so the substance of charitable men often, if not ordinarily, multiplies in the very giving out of it, even as the five loaves and few fish multiplied in the breaking and distributing of them. And the widow’s oil increased by being poured out.

But these bare assertions are not full proof. So, to clear the truth of this doctrine better, I will prove it by #emph[Scriptures, examples and reasons.]
